\begin_inset Formula $z_{n}=f_{\lambda,\mu}^{n}(c_{1})$
\end_inset

,
 considering that 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{n}$
\end_inset

 escapes when 
\begin_inset Formula $|z_{n}-f_{\lambda,\mu}^{2}(z_{n})|<10^{-6}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Re(f_{\lambda,\mu}(z_{n}))<-16$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The family 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\lambda,\mu}$
\end_inset

 does not have a unique set of critical points for all pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $(\lambda,\mu)$
\end_inset

;
 there are infinitely many critical points for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(\lambda,\mu)$
\end_inset

,
 and none of them can be written explicitly in a formula.
 Thus,
 the drawing of an analogous Mandelbrot set cannot directly use the classical drawing algorithms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First,
 it is necessary to calculate the critical points.
 Let us recall that the critical points 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{k}=c_{k}(\lambda,\mu)$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\lambda,\mu}$
\end_inset

 corresponds to the intersections of the curves
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{\pm}(x)=\pm\sqrt{\left|\frac{\mu}{\lambda}\right|\frac{1}{e^{x}}-x^{2}},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x(y)=-\frac{y}{\tan(\frac{y+\pi}{2})}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the following items,
 we describe the algorithms to approximate numerically the critical points 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{k}=c_{k}(\lambda,\mu)$
\end_inset

,
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in\mathbb{N}-\left\{ 0\right\} $
\end_inset

,
 given a parameter pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(\lambda,\mu)\in\mathbb{R}^{-}\times\mathbb{R}^{+}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Approximation of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 the nearest critical point to 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $Re(c_{1})>0$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
We know that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the intersection of the curves 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{+}(x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(y)$
\end_inset

,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in(0,\pi)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}$
\end_inset

 is approximated using a bisection search algorithm over 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 with initial values 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{0,\min}=\delta=0.001$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{0,\max}=\pi-\delta$
\end_inset

,
 stopping when 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\frac{\mu}{\lambda}\right|\frac{1}{e^{x_{n}}}-x_{n}^{2}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $|f'_{\lambda,\mu}(x_{n}+y_{+}(x_{n})i)|^{2}<\varepsilon=10^{-8}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{n}=x(y_{n})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Approximation of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{k}$
\end_inset

,
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k>1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
First,
 using the Newton-Raphson algorithm,
 we approximate the root 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{L}<0$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $g(x)=\left|\frac{\mu}{\lambda}\right|\frac{1}{e^{x}}-x^{2}$
\end_inset

 such that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in(-\infty,x_{L})$
\end_inset

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $g(x)>0$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{+}(x)=\sqrt{g(x)}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{+}(x_{L})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We know that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{k}\in(-\infty,x_{L})\times((2k-3)\pi,(2k-1)\pi)$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{k}$
\end_inset

 is approximated using a compass search algorithm with initial value 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{0}=x_{L}+2(k-1)\pi i$
\end_inset

,
 stopping when 
\begin_inset Formula $|f'_{\lambda,\mu}(z_{n})|^{2}<\varepsilon=10^{-8}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now,
 it can be defined the analogue of the Mandelbrot set for the family 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\lambda,\mu}$
\end_inset

 (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:M1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

